/*
  Speed.strings
  TeslaSync — P4 shared surface · 0088 · Speed (Apple)

  The P1/S10 i18n catalog contribution for this surface. The web source
  (components/data-display/format/Speed.tsx) renders NO translatable copy: it
  makes no `t()` call. It displays only a speed figure — a caller-supplied
  `mph` / `kmh` converted into the user's preferred display unit — suffixed
  with that unit's label, or, for a no-value input, a locale-neutral em dash
  (the web `<span>—</span>` fallback). There are therefore no message keys to
  mirror, and the Swift sources hold no English literals.

  The unit labels ("mph" / "km/h") are NOT translatable copy: they are
  universal unit symbols carried verbatim from the user's preference
  (`useUnits().unitPrefs.speed`, derived from `settings.unit_of_length`),
  exactly as the currency symbol is in the sibling 0083 Currency surface and
  the SI unit symbols are across the unit-conversion layer. They are rendered
  with `Text(verbatim:)` so they are never reinterpreted as localization keys.

  The surface's only locale-sensitive output is the number itself: grouping
  separators and the decimal mark. That localization is bound through the
  injected `Locale` (SpeedInput.locale → SpeedFormatting.number), the native
  parity of the web `fmtNumber` global-locale read, rather than through a
  string table. The title (tooltip) figure mirrors `value.toFixed(1)` and is
  intentionally locale-neutral. The per-surface table name ("Speed") is
  reserved here so the file-set stays consistent with its sibling surfaces and
  folds into the app `Localizable.xcstrings` master catalog at integration
  time should localizable chrome ever be added.
*/
